==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|adoun}}, from {{inh|en|ang|adūn}}, earlier {{m|ang|ofdūne|t=down}}, from {{m|ang|of}} {{m|ang|dun|dūne|off the hill}} (compare {{noncog|la|ad vallum}} > {{noncog|fro|à val}}, used in the same way).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈdaʊn/}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[down|Down]], [[downward]]; to or in a lower place.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=VII|stanza=24|passage=Thrice did she sink '''adown'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|author=Ferna Vale|title=Natalie; or, A Gem Among the Sea-Weeds|text=Many a family circle wept as they looked upon the familiar places, which would know their lost ones no more; but ah, chide me not, kind reader, in thus leading you '''adown''' to the coldness of death, in setting before you that which causes your tender heart to shudder.}}

